# Engine seams that no production code outside their own package calls, and are
# `pub` anyway. Read by check_engine_seams.sh: a seam with no such caller that
# is not listed here fails the build, and a listed one that gains a caller (or
# goes away) fails too, so the list cannot quietly outlive its reasons.
#
# The bar is not "a test needs it". A test that needs to reach inside a package
# can be a whitebox test, and a symbol only its own package's tests want is dead
# weight in a production source file — the fix there is to delete it and assert
# through what production actually uses. What earns a line here is a symbol
# whose entire purpose is to be *asserted*, by tests in a different package that
# have no other way to see what they are checking.
#
# Format: pkg/Symbol — reason
frame/DataFrame::check_invariants — the structural check itself (INV1–INV7); the frame, io and lazy suites all assert that a frame they built is well-formed, and nothing else can see a private field
series/Series::is_canonical — the backend-canonicalisation invariant; the operators that must return a canonical column are series' own row rebuilds (gather / gather_series / drop_nulls / try_column_to_numeric), and series' blackbox tests are a separate package that can only assert it if the predicate is pub
series/Series::storage_kind — the only way a test outside series can see which backend an operator's output landed on, which is engine-wide behaviour worth pinning
